#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# empirical_distribution

#% text
# `empirical_distribution(data)` is the distribution that takes each value of `data` with the relative frequency it has in `data`.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# `empirical_distribution(weights -> data)` gives the values the given weights (normalized to total 1).

#% item
# `pdf`, `cdf`, `mean`, `variance`, `standard_deviation`, `quantile`, `median` and `random_variate` work on it; the variance is the population variance (division by the number of values).

#% item
# The distribution is an inert term; the data may be exact numbers or symbols that compare.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
mean(empirical_distribution([1, 2, 2, 5]))
#% output
#= 5/2

#% cell
variance(empirical_distribution([1, 2, 2, 5]))
#% output
#= 9/4

#% cell
pdf(empirical_distribution([1, 2, 2, 5]), 2)
#% output
#= 1/2

#% cell
cdf(empirical_distribution([1, 2, 2, 5]), 3)
#% output
#= 3/4

#% cell
quantile(empirical_distribution([1, 2, 2, 5]), 3/4)
#% output
#= 2

#% cell
mean(empirical_distribution([1, 3] -> [10, 20]))
#% output
#= 35/2

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `pdf`, `cdf`, `quantile`, `random_variate`, `mean`
